  "faithful": "HELLEBORUS, HELLEBORR, in botany, a genus of the polyandria polygynia clasfs. It has no calix; the petals are five or more; the nectarium is tubular and bilabiated; and the capsule contains many feeds. There are five species, two of them natives of Britain, viz. the fetidus, or great balfard black hellebore; and the viridis, or wild black hellebore. The hellebore, when taken in large quantities, is poisonous; but the root, in small doses, is supposed to attenuate the humours, and to promote urinary and uterine discharges.",
  "modern": "HELLEBORUS, HELLEBORR, in botany, a genus of the polyandria polygynia class. It has no calix; the petals are five or more; the nectarium is tubular and bilabiated; and the capsule contains many feeds. There are five species, two of them natives of Britain, viz. the fetidus, or great balfard black hellebore; and the viridis, or wild black hellebore. The hellebore, when taken in large quantities, is poisonous; but the root, in small doses, is supposed to attenuate the humours, and to promote urinary and uterine discharges."
